latest_plan = 'Based on the reflection, here\'s a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Identify Colored Centers:\n   - Scan the input grid for non-green, non-black cells that are adjacent to green cells.\n   - Store these as "colored centers" with their coordinates and color values.\n\n2. Handle Empty Grid Case:\n   - If no colored centers are found, use the highest color value in the grid as a default center at (0, 0).\n\n3. Sort Colored Centers:\n   - Sort the list of colored centers by their color value in descending order.\n   This ensures that higher-valued colors take precedence during expansion.\n\n4. Create Vertical Lines:\n   - For each colored center, create a vertical line from top to bottom of the grid.\n   - When encountering an existing color, only overwrite if the new color is higher-valued.\n\n5. Create Horizontal Lines:\n   - For each colored center, create horizontal lines extending left and right.\n   - Stop expansion when encountering a higher or equal valued color.\n\n6. Fill Territories:\n   - Scan each row of the grid from left to right.\n   - Fill empty (black) cells between colored cells with the color of the nearest left non-empty cell.\n\n7. Preserve Original Green Cells:\n   - Iterate through the input grid and copy all green cells to their corresponding positions in the new grid.\n\n8. Remove Isolated Green Cells:\n   - Scan for green cells that have no adjacent green cells.\n   - Replace these isolated green cells with the highest adjacent color value.\n\n9. Fill Remaining Interior Black Cells:\n   - Scan all non-edge cells of the grid.\n   - For any remaining black cells, replace them with the highest adjacent color value.\n\n10. Final Check:\n    - Ensure that all non-edge cells have a non-black color.\n    - Edge cells are allowed to remain black.\n\nThis plan addresses all the observed patterns and behaviors across the different test cases. It accounts for the color hierarchy, the special behavior of green cells, the expansion of colored territories, and the filling of remaining spaces. By following these steps, the algorithm should be able to handle all the variations seen in the test cases, from grids with multiple competing colors to those with only a single expanding color.'
